Quick GitHub Repo Setup

Optional: configure Git user globally (PowerShell, Bash, or other terminal)
   - git config --global user.name "mahesh"
   - git config --global user.email "maheshgowdamahi641@gmail.com"
   - git config --global --list

1) Create local repo
   - Open terminal in your project folder (PowerShell, Git Bash, WSL, or another shell):
     git init
   - Add files:
     git add .
   - Commit:
     git commit -m "Initial commit"

2) Create GitHub repo
   - Go to https://github.com
   - Click New repository
   - Enter repo name and optional description
   - Choose Public or Private
   - Click Create repository

3) Connect local repo to GitHub
   - git remote add origin https://github.com/maheshkumar-sj/pesce.git
   - git branch -M main
   - git push -u origin main

Second Time Pushing Code

1) Make changes
   - Edit files in your project.

2) Stage and commit
   - git add .
   - git commit -m "Describe your changes"

3) Push again
   - git push

Notes

- Check the repo status before staging/committing:
  git status
- View configured Git settings:
  git config --list
- To check remote:
  git remote -v
- If GitHub asks for authentication, use a GitHub token or SSH depending on your setup.
- For SSH instead of HTTPS:
  git remote add origin git@github.com:<user>/<repo>.git
